<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  THE RELEASE POLICY. Debug and profile builds override this file with a
  permissive one (see src/debug and src/profile), so `flutter run` is unaffected
  by anything here.

  This replaces `android:usesCleartextTraffic="true"` on <application>, which
  permitted plain http to EVERY host on the internet for the sake of one LAN
  backend. Narrowing it does not make cleartext safe — a bearer token on plain
  http is readable by anyone on the same network, and only TLS fixes that — but
  it does mean a shipped build cannot be talked into sending one anywhere
  except the addresses named below.

  WHY THESE THREE. 127.0.0.1 and localhost are the device itself, unreachable
  from any other machine. 10.0.2.2 is the Android emulator's alias for the host
  it runs on, and it is the app's built-in default base URL — without it a
  release build on an emulator fails every request out of the box, with a
  connection error that says nothing about why.

  Note the one imperfection, stated rather than hidden: on a PHYSICAL device
  10.0.2.2 is an ordinary private address rather than an emulator alias, so this
  entry is slightly wider there than it reads. It is kept because the emulator
  case is common and the alternative is a build that silently cannot talk to
  anything.

  RUNNING A RELEASE BUILD AGAINST YOUR OWN http:// BACKEND: add its host below.
  It has to be a literal host — Android's network security config matches
  hostnames and IP literals, never CIDR ranges, so "my LAN" cannot be expressed.
  That limitation is the reason this is a build-time decision and not a Settings
  toggle: the app lets you change the server URL at runtime, but the platform
  decides what cleartext is allowed before the app starts.

      <domain includeSubdomains="false">192.0.2.20</domain>

  (192.0.2.x is the RFC 5737 documentation range, chosen so that grepping this
  file for "domain" cannot make the example look like a live entry. Replace it
  with your actual address.)

  The real fix is to put TLS in front of the backend and use https://, after
  which nothing needs listing here at all.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false" />

    <domain-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">127.0.0.1</domain>
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">localhost</domain>
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">10.0.2.2</domain>
    </domain-config>
</network-security-config>
